      </p>

      <h2 class="mt-3 text-4xl font-bold tracking-tight sm:text-5xl">
        The project in one minute
      </h2>

      <div class="mt-8 max-w-4xl space-y-6 text-lg leading-8 text-muted">

        <p>
          Basically, I built a telecom customer churn prediction system
          to identify customers who are likely to leave the company.
          I used the IBM Telco Customer Churn dataset with 7,043 customers
          and 33 columns initially.
        </p>

        <p>
          I cleaned the data, removed identifiers and columns that could
          cause leakage, encoded the categorical variables, and split the
          data into 80% training and 20% testing using stratification.
        </p>

        <p>
          I compared Logistic Regression, Random Forest and XGBoost.
          XGBoost performed best initially with 80.62% accuracy and
          85.57% ROC-AUC.
        </p>

        <p>
          But accuracy wasn't the main business objective. At the default
          0.50 threshold, the model was missing 164 actual churners.
        </p>

        <p>
          So I tested different probability thresholds and selected 0.30
          for this project. That increased recall from 56.15% to 78.34%
          and reduced false negatives from 164 to 81, although false
          positives increased.
        </p>

        <p>
          Then I used SHAP to understand what the model was relying on.
          Contract type, tenure, dependents and fiber-optic service were
          among the most influential features.
        </p>

        <p>
          Finally, I converted churn probabilities into risk levels and
          recommended retention actions, so the output wasn't just
          "churn" or "not churn" but a prioritized customer-risk workflow.
        </p>

      </div>

    </div>

  </section>


